ith only ~20 AIRs each. Most of APC 300's AIRs are small autoprecompile-generated ones, whereas APC 0's AIRs are larger and fewer. Breaking down APC 300's per-segment count: roughly 291 autoprecompile AIRs and 20 non-APC AIRs (which are the same ones as in APC 0 but possibly smaller since work shifted to autoprecompiles). The autoprecompile AIRs are the extra ones driving the per-AI overhead.\n\nI'm wondering if these autoprecompile AIRs could be processed more efficiently since they likely share uniform structure—similar constraint patterns, small height, few columns, and simple interaction expressions. If many of them implement the same operation like field multiplication with identical constraint polynomials and interaction expressions, they could potentially be batched into a single kernel launch where each thread block handles one AIR's data instead of launching separate kernels for each.\n\nThe key difference from previous batching attempts is that same-structure AIRs share the same DAG and rules without per-AIR intermediate diversity, and since each AIR's intermediates are small (around 40KB), batching a hundred of them stays within L2 cache limits while eliminating the kernel dispatch overhead entirely. The real challenge is detecting which AIRs share the same structure at runtime—the autoprecompile system might already group them by type, but I need to check how they're identified in the codebase.\n\nLooking at this from the kernel side, the current evaluation kernel receives per-AIR parameters like trace pointers, DAG rules, and intermediate buffers as arguments. To batch multiple AIRs, I'd need to pass arrays of these parameters instead, which is what the previous batching attempt tried but ran into issues with aggregate overhead.\n\nThe report shows that without a memory budget cap, batching caused severe regression because the aggregate intermediates for ~211 AIRs (around 576MB total) vastly exceeded the 72MB L2 cache, destroying the cache reuse that makes the sequential per-stream approach fast. With a 64MB L2 budget cap in place, batching gets disabled for all significant workloads. But if I only batch AIRs with small intermediates—say under 100KB each—I could fit around 720 of them within the budget.\n\nFor small autoprecompile AIRs with height 1024 and ~10 intermediate nodes, the intermediates would be only about 40KB, allowing me to batch up to 1800 AIRs within L2. However, the real bottleneck is that the batched kernel still needs to read all the trace data for each AIR. Even with L2-fitting intermediates, 200 small AIRs with 640KB of trace each would mean 128MB of trace data—exceeding L2 and thrashing the cache. The sequential per-stream approach avoids this because only one AIR per stream is active at a time, keeping the aggregate trace data in flight to just 5MB across 8 streams, but batching would load all that trace data simultaneously.\n\nThe per-stream sequential approach gets L2 reuse by processing each AIR's trace data completely before moving to the next one, keeping the L2 footprint bounded by the largest single AIR. Batching destroys this benefit by processing many AIRs at once, making the L2 footprint the sum of all batched AIRs' trace data plus intermediates. So even though the intermediates fit, the trace data doesn't—batching small AIRs for GKR input eval isn't viable.\n\nI'm starting to accept that reducing the per-AIR overhead further with the current approach is difficult. The multi-stream plus pre-allocation plus GPU strategy seems like the best path forward.\n\nBut wait—what about optimizing the GKR inner rounds themselves? At APC 300, they take 213ms total across two segments, which is still the biggest bottleneck even though they scale well from 592ms at APC 0. The main kernels doing the work are the round computation and bit reversal operations for fractional sumcheck, each taking tens of milliseconds per segment.\n\nThe CPU work between rounds is minimal—just copying results back, re