ing zero hashes at different tree levels. The kernel might already be optimized to cache the zero hash rather than recomputing it, but the overhead of 106ms across all these calls is still noticeable.\n\nThe real insight is that for a fixed hash function like Poseidon2, the zero hash at each tree level is deterministic — level 0 is just the hash of a zero row, level 1 is the hash of two level-0 zero hashes, and so on. So theoretically these could be precomputed once and reused across all trees, though different tree depths and widths would require different zero hash chains. - Level k: hash of (zero_hash_{k-1}, zero_hash_{k-1})\n\nThese are deterministic given the Poseidon2 parameters. If we precomputed them (17 hashes total), we'd eliminate ALL 584 kernel launches. But the kernel takes width as input — different widths produce different zero hashes at level 0.\n\nIf different trees have different row widths, the level-0 zero hash differs, and all subsequent levels differ too. With 106K columns at APC 300, the row width is very large. But at different APC configs, the row width differs.\n\nWait, within one benchmark run, there might be multiple trees with different widths:\n- Trace Commit: width = total stacked columns (~106K at APC 300)\n- WHIR rounds: width varies (decreasing at each round due to folding)\n\nSo different trees have different widths, requiring different zero hashes. Within the same run, each width gets reused across multiple trees (one per segment), so precomputing zero hashes for each unique width would cut down from 584 calls to roughly 34, saving around 100ms. But 182μs per call for a simple hash seems excessive — a Poseidon2 permutation on GPU should take ~1μs. The function signature suggests it's absorbing a 106K-wide row of zeros, which might explain the overhead.\n\nThe striking observation is that all 584 calls have nearly identical runtimes (182μs ± 0.6μs), indicating they're all computing the same hash with the same width. This is a major