 and if it's the former, caching eliminates all that waste.\n\nNow I need to figure out whether those 584 calls span the entire benchmark run including recursion proofs, or just the app proof itself. The nsight profile captures everything, so if recursion is included, I'm looking at leaf and internal recursion proofs with their own tree configurations. Breaking it down: the app proof has 2 segments with their Trace Commit and WHIR commitments, leaf recursion adds another segment, and internal recursion contributes multiple smaller proofs. That could push the total to around 34 trees (584 divided by 17 levels), but the actual savings depend on whether the cache persists across all proofs or just within the app proof's STARK measurement.\n\nLet me focus on just the app proof's calculate_zero_hash calls. With 2 segments across roughly 6 WHIR rounds at 17 levels each, I'm looking at around 204 calls from WHIR alone, plus the 34 from Trace Commit, totaling about 238 calls. At 182 microseconds each, that's roughly 43 milliseconds—which is less than the full 106 milliseconds measured, meaning the remaining 346 calls come from recursion proofs. The real question is how many unique (width, level) combinations exist within the app proof itself. Trace Commit gives me 17 unique combinations at one width, WHIR contributes around 102 unique combinations across its 6 rounds with decreasing widths, so I'm looking at roughly 119 unique configurations total.\n\nThat means out of 238 calls, about 119 are redundant—caching those would save roughly 22 milliseconds. It's meaningful but only about 2% of the 1125 millisecond STARK time, so it won't solve the performance issue on its own. I need to think about whether extending the cache across the entire proof, including recursion proofs, would yield more substantial gains. Let me also recalculate what happens at APC 0, where the number of segments increases to 5, which would push the calculate_zero_hash calls to around 595 total. ~7μs per