till running? That would overlap compute with hashing. If NTT takes 40ms and hashing takes 58ms and they fully overlap, I'd get max(40, 58) = 58ms instead of 98ms—a 40ms savings per segment, 80ms total. That's significant, but it requires restructuring the pipeline since batch_ntt operates on the entire matrix at once, and the Merkle tree needs all the data before it can complete.\n\nActually, wait—the hash absorbs all columns of a row at once, so I can't start hashing until every column in that row is NTT'd. That means the NTT has to finish completely before any hashing can begin. No overlap possible. I could try hashing columns instead of rows, but that breaks how Merkle trees typically work. That's a dead end.\n\nLet me look at reducing the RS codeword computation cost instead. The batch_expand_pad operation zero-pads the stacked matrix from its current height up to a larger height, and that's where a lot of the overhead is coming from.\n\nNow I'm looking at the subsequent operations—the iNTT within chunks, the subset-zeta transform, bit-reversal, and the full NTT. These all operate on the entire matrix, and with 106K columns at APC 300, that's a lot of data to process. Each column gets transformed independently, so the width of the matrix matters. The column height should be consistent across all columns since it's determined by the maximum expanded height across all AIRs in the segment, though at APC 300 with many smaller AIRs, that max height might actually be lower than at APC 0.\n\nBut the per-segment Trace Commit timing is around 98ms at both APC 0 and APC 300, which suggests the total cell count per segment is similar despite the different matrix dimensions. That makes sense—the product of height and width stays roughly constant.\n\nI don't think I can meaningfully reduce the RS codeword computation without fundamental algorithmic changes. The Merkle tree depth is tied to the query stride, which depends on the matrix height rather than width, so that's also similar across both configurations. I'm going to commit to my best optimization approach after exploring all these angles.\n\nAfter 44 iterations, I'm looking at the remaining 231ms gap broken down by component: the Round 0 per-AIR overhead accounts for about 105ms, MLE Rounds per-trace overhead adds another 102ms, Stacked Reduction contributes 35ms, with minor compensations elsewhere. The root cause is having 6.3x more AIRs at APC 300 while maintaining similar per-segment cell counts. The per-AIR overhead is dominated by GPU kernel compute time rather than API overhead, so reducing the number of kernels or the data they process would be the most impactful lever. I'm noticing the `precompute_logup_combinations` function has 791 instances of a particular variant, which might be worth examining more closely.\n\nLooking at the kernel launch patterns, there are roughly 1582 total launches across both variants contributing about 55ms of GPU time. The kernel precomputes logup combination values for the MLE sumcheck at the start of each round, and with 28 round-segment pairs at APC 300, that works out to around 56 launches per pair—essentially one per trace. The two variants likely correspond to different trace evaluation modes, each with about 28 launches per round-segment, which seems like a significant number of redundant kernel invocations.\n\nI'm thinking about batching these precompute_logup_combinations launches into descriptor-array batches, similar to how the batch_mle kernels operate. Instead of launching 56 separate kernels per round-segment, I could consolidate down to just 2 batched launches—one for each variant. That would drop the total from 1582 launches to 56, keeping the 55ms of actual GPU compute but slashing launch overhead from roughly 8ms down to 0.3ms. The savings aren't huge, but it's worth exploring whether batching could also improve GPU utilization by letting more work execute concurrently.\n\nActually, I'm realizing the real bottleneck might be elsewhere. What if instead of optimizing kernel launches, I focus on reducing the number of MLE rounds themselves? At APC 300, we're running about 14 rounds determined by the maximum log_height across all AIRs. If I could lower that n_max value, I'd save time across the entire elimination process.\n\nBut wait — n_max is fixed by the protocol. The sumcheck needs to reduce over all variables, so I can't change this constraint.\n\nLet me look at the per-round costs more carefully. Each round-segment pair takes about 5.75ms, broken down into precompute_logup_combinations (~2ms), batch MLE evaluation (~1.7ms), monomial evaluation (~1.3ms), interpolation (~0.4ms), and reduction (~0.3ms). The GPU is essentially running continuously here since it's single-threaded, so the wall time matches the compute time.\n\nThe real opportunity is in the dominant costs — precompute_logup_combinations, batch MLE eval, and monomial eval together account for about 5ms per round. If I could speed up these kernels or find a way to parallelize the work differently...\n\nActually, I already tried multi-streaming the MLE rounds across separate threads with different CUDA streams, but it didn't help because the GPU compute itself is the bottleneck, not the scheduling. So that's a dead end.\n\nLet me step back and look at Trace Commit differently — that's where the real time is going. At APC 300, it's taking 196ms total across both segments, and the stack_columns_kernel alone is showing up multiple times in the profile.\n\nNow I'm breaking down all the kernels involved: the bit reversal permutation, the forward and inverse NTT operations, the smaller batch NTT calls, and the 2D interpolation kernel are all contributing. The poseidon2 hashing is bundled into a larger operation, but these individual kernels are each running dozens of times with relatively small per-call costs that add up.\n\nLooking more carefully at what's actually happening in Trace Commit specifically, I realize the NTT kernels