ccount for about 321ms combined across both segments, so if the zero hash calls scale proportionally with that work, I can estimate how many are actually relevant to the bottleneck I'm trying to address.\n\nBased on the timing breakdown, the app proof represents roughly 73% of the total Trace Commit and WHIR work, which would put its share of the calculate_zero_hash calls at around 77ms—a meaningful chunk that could potentially be optimized through caching. Now I need to understand what calculate_zero_hash actually does in the Merkle tree implementation to see if caching is even feasible.\n\nLooking at the Merkle tree code, the leaf hashing uses poseidon2_compressing_row_hashes followed by a layer compression loop with merkle_tree_compress, but calculate_zero_hash doesn't appear in that flow. It's likely being used to compute hashes for zero-padded rows that extend the trace data up to the next power-of-2 tree size, or to generate empty subtree hashes for sparse structures.\n\nSince the stacked matrix at APC 300 might only have 65K actual rows but needs to fill a 2^17 = 131K leaf tree, those extra 66K zero-padded rows would have precomputed zero hashes at each level that can be reused during construction instead of recalculating them repeatedly. Let me check the actual implementation to verify this.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3764,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10413,"cache_read_input_tokens":114325,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":10413},"output_tokens":42,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"78bbe79a-49aa-4ea3-b7e0-2913b403757d","uuid":"ec650cff-3c33-4dcf-b9e7-1a4b606a1c84"}
