s—STARK excl trace is 1.12s while Set Initial Memory is only 0.13s. So my focus on calculate_zero_hash as an optimization target was misguided.\n\nLet me refocus on what's actually within STARK excl trace by examining the GPU kernels more carefully. When I sum up the individual kernel times—Round 0, GKR input eval, GKR inner, MLE, Trace Commit, and Openings—I get around 2389ms total, but the wall time is only 1125ms. That gap comes from multi-streaming parallelization, particularly with Round 0 and GKR input eval running across 8 threads. Now I need to recalculate the actual wall-time GPU contribution by accounting for this parallelization.\n\nBreaking down each phase by dividing GPU time by the parallelism factor: Round 0's 675ms becomes roughly 193ms wall time, GKR input eval's 526ms becomes 155ms, GKR inner stays around 213ms since it's mostly sequential, MLE is 161ms, and Trace Commit's 686ms includes some WHIR and recursion overhead so the actual contribution is closer to 196ms wall time. Adding in WHIR at 125ms and Stacked Reduction at 74ms, the total comes to about 1119ms, which matches the observed 1125ms wall time.\n\nNow I'm reconsidering the breakdown—GKR's 369ms total is already split into input eval (156ms) and inner rounds (213ms), so I shouldn't double-count those. The real bottleneck is the GKR inner rounds at 213ms, which are inherently sequential due to Fiat-Shamir dependencies and can't be parallelized, but I'm wondering if there's still room to optimize them further.\n\nLooking at the per-segment kernel costs, I'm seeing that precompute_m, compute_round_and_revert, multifold, and bit_rev_frac_build_k2 together account for roughly 106ms per segment, which matches the observed 107ms. The question is whether I can fuse some of these kernels—specifically precompute_m and multifold—since precompute_m's output feeds directly into multifold's input, so combining them might eliminate intermediate memory transfers and reduce overhead.\n\nHowever, they don't actually run back-to-back in practice. Between precompute_m and multifold, there are three eval_round calls that involve GPU-CPU synchronization, so the data dependency chain breaks the opportunity for fusion. When I trace through the actual execution flow per window—precompute_m takes about 3.2ms, then three eval rounds at roughly 0.5ms each, then multifold at 3.4ms—I get around 8.1ms per window. With roughly 7 layers per segment and one window per layer, that's about 57ms, but I'm observing 107ms total, so the remaining 50ms is coming from bit_rev_frac_build_k2 and compute_round_and_revert, which together account for the gap.\n\nRather than getting stuck in the weeds of per-segment accounting, the real insight is that the GKR inner rounds are fundamentally sequential and hard to parallelize. So I should shift focus to what actually can be optimized—specifically, whether there are idle GPU periods between different phases where I could overlap work, like between the trace commit and GKR phases.\n\nThe transitions between phases are CPU-only and happen in microseconds, so there's not much slack there. But within individual phases, there might be gaps—like between NTT and Merkle hashing in trace commit, or between device-to-host transfers and the next kernel launch in GKR. The D2H overhead per round is small, but with so many round-layer-segment combinations across all the GKR layers, it's hard to know if this actually matters without diving deeper into the exact structure.\n\nLet me step back and look at this from a completely different angle.\n\nAfter 44 iterations of optimization, the per-AIR overhead work has mostly been exhausted. The STARK excluding trace is still at 1125ms at APC 300, but the target is 894ms. The core problem is that APC 300 has 6.3x more AIRs per segment than APC 0, and costs like kernel launches and buffer setup scale with AIR count. We've chipped away at the per-AIR costs, but there's a floor we can't go below.\n\nWhat if instead of continuing to optimize per-AIR costs, I rethink the whole approach and batch the small autoprecompile AIRs together to avoid paying those costs multiple times?\n\nThe idea is to group small AIRs by trace height, merge their trace columns and constraint DAGs into single unified structures, then process each group with one set of kernel launches. This would collapse ~583 autoprecompile AIRs down to maybe 10-20 height groups, slashing the per-AIR overhead. The merged trace has more columns but the intermediates buffer stays proportional to the row count and total DAG nodes, not the number of separate AIRs. I'm realizing this might actually be equivalent to what I tried before, so I need to think through whether there's a real difference here.\n\nActually, merging at the DAG level doesn't solve the problem either—each thread would still need to sequentially evaluate constraints from different AIRs on their respective columns, which just shifts the work from kernel launches to within-thread computation without reducing the total intermediates buffer size or the fundamental bottleneck.\n\nA mega-kernel that processes all small AIRs in a single launch with descriptor arrays could work in theory, but the real constraint is L2 cache capacity—different thread blocks reading different AIRs' traces causes the same thrashing. The key insight is that we could artificially limit concurrent thread blocks using `maxBlocksPerSM=1` or cooperative groups to ensure only one AIR's data fits in L2 at a time, effectively serializing the work but keeping memory access patterns cache-friendly.\n\nHowever, this doesn't actually reduce total GPU time—it just controls concurrency. The real win would be eliminating per-AIR kernel launch overhead (~5μs × 583 AIRs ≈ 2.9ms), which is negligible. I'm starting to accept that no single optimization will hit the 231ms target, so I need to focus on the best available approach even if it falls short.\n\nLet me shift focus to WHIR, which is regressed to 125ms