erlap the final phase of seg 0 (WHIR opening at 65ms) with the initial phase of seg 1 (Trace Commit at 89ms)? These operations use different GPU buffers — WHIR reads from seg 0's cached RS matrix while Trace Commit writes to seg 1's stacked matrix — so they shouldn't directly conflict. If I launch seg 1's Trace Commit on a background thread while seg 0's WHIR is still running, they could overlap on the GPU. The theoretical savings would be up to 65ms, though GPU contention would probably reduce that to around 30-50ms in practice. This looks like the most impactful optimization I can pursue.\n\nThe implementation strategy is to break the segment loop into phases, spawn a background thread for seg 1's Trace Commit after seg 0's Constraints phase finishes, and continue seg 0's Openings on the main thread. This requires managing concurrent CUDA streams and handling concurrent segment allocations in VPMM, but it follows the multi-stream pattern already established elsewhere in the code. I'm going to go with this approach — overlapping seg N's WHIR opening with seg N+1's Trace Commit via multi-threading.\n\nWait, there's a correctness issue I need to think through with seg 1...\n\nActually, I'm realizing each segment has its own independent Fiat-Shamir transcript, and the cross-segment challenge is only generated after all segments complete. So seg 1's Trace Commit doesn't depend on seg 0's transcript state at all — they can run concurrently. Let me verify this is actually how the code is structured by looking at the coordinator's segment processing loop.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2824,"cache_read_input_tokens":190329,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2824},"output_tokens":11,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"78bbe79a-49aa-4ea3-b7e0-2