{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01EDtU2fXVwvYdgN42EpoMuJ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_014THYRU7XWTgpXrxPdHTUgY","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/task.md","content":"# 2026-04-16-1100-whir-vpmm-arena-fold-buffers\n\nRecover the ~25ms WHIR regression at APC 300 by allocating WHIR sumcheck fold buffers from a VPMM-backed arena instead of cudaMallocAsync. When the VPMM page size was increased from 2 MiB to 16 MiB (task vpmm-bulk-page-creation), WHIR's 2-4 MiB fold buffers fell below the page_size threshold and were routed to cudaMallocAsync instead of VPMM. This changed the physical memory layout for subsequent bandwidth-sensitive GPU kernels. Previous attempts to fix this with global page size tuning (tune-vpmm-page-size-for-whir, decouple-vpmm-pool-threshold) failed because any global change that helped WHIR caused a larger GKR regression. A WHIR-specific arena allocates one 16+ MiB VPMM-backed buffer before the fold loop, then sub-allocates fold buffers as non-owning views into this arena, restoring VPMM-managed physical memory layout for WHIR without affecting any other phase's allocation patterns.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":19731,"cache_read_input_tokens":193153,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":19731},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"78bbe79a-49aa-4ea3-b7e0-2913b403757d","uuid":"343a6fae-9993-41a3-94c7-6b1f2835d55c"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014THYRU7XWTgpXrxPdHTUgY","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/task.md has been updated successfully."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"78bbe79a-49aa-4ea3-b7e0-2913b403757d","uuid":"bed7f372-77b4-475c-8126-5b601c89e81f","timestamp":"2026-04-16T09:25:08.617Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"update","filePath":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/task.md","content":"# 2026-04-16-1100-whir-vpmm-arena-fold-buffers\n\nRecover the ~25ms WHIR regression at APC 300 by allocating WHIR sumcheck fold buffers from a VPMM-backed arena instead of cudaMallocAsync. When the VPMM page size was increased from 2 MiB to 16 MiB (task vpmm-bulk-page-creation), WHIR's 2-4 MiB fold buffers fell below the page_size threshold and were routed to cudaMallocAsync instead of VPMM. This changed the physical memory layout for subsequent bandwidth-sensitive GPU kernels. Previous attempts to fix this with global page size tuning (tune-vpmm-page-size-for-whir, decouple-vpmm-pool-threshold) failed because any global change that helped WHIR caused a larger GKR regression. A WHIR-specific arena allocates one 16+ MiB VPMM-backed buffer before the fold loop, then sub-allocates fold buffers as non-owning views into this arena, restoring VPMM-managed physical memory layout for WHIR without affecting any other phase's allocation patterns.\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":1,"oldLines":3,"newStart":1,"newLines":3,"lines":["-# 2026-04-16-1100-inline-logup-combination-precompute","+# 2026-04-16-1100-whir-vpmm-arena-fold-buffers"," ","-Inline the `precompute_logup_combinations` GPU kernel computation into the `logup_monomial_kernel` to eliminate 1582 redundant kernel launches and ~55ms of separate GPU compute per proof. Currently, each MLE sumcheck round launches separate precompute kernels that compute `sum_t(eq_3bs[interaction_idx_t] * coefficient_t)` for every monomial, writing to intermediate buffers that the monomial evaluation kernel then reads. Since the monomial kernel already accesses the MonomialHeader (which contains `num_terms`/`term_offset`) and the required data (`d_eq_3bs`, `d_terms`) is already on the device, the combination can be computed inline with negligible overhead (~2-4 FpExt multiply-adds per monomial), eliminating the intermediate buffer allocation, the H2D/D2H traffic, and the precompute kernel launches entirely.","+Recover the ~25ms WHIR regression at APC 300 by allocating WHIR sumcheck fold buffers from a VPMM-backed arena instead of cudaMallocAsync. When the VPMM page size was increased from 2 MiB to 16 MiB (task vpmm-bulk-page-creation), WHIR's 2-4 MiB fold buffers fell below the page_size threshold and were routed to cudaMallocAsync instead of VPMM. This changed the physical memory layout for subsequent bandwidth-sensitive GPU kernels. Previous attempts to fix this with global page size tuning (tune-vpmm-page-size-for-whir, decouple-vpmm-pool-threshold) failed because any global change that helped WHIR caused a larger GKR regression. A WHIR-specific arena allocates one 16+ MiB VPMM-backed buffer before the fold loop, then sub-allocates fold buffers as non-owning views into this arena, restoring VPMM-managed physical memory layout for WHIR without affecting any other phase's allocation patterns."]}],"originalFile":"# 2026-04-16-1100-inline-logup-combination-precompute\n\nInline the `precompute_logup_combinations` GPU kernel computation into the `logup_monomial_kernel` to eliminate 1582 redundant kernel launches and ~55ms of separate GPU compute per proof. Currently, each MLE sumcheck round launches separate precompute kernels that compute `sum_t(eq_3bs[interaction_idx_t] * coefficient_t)` for every monomial, writing to intermediate buffers that the monomial evaluation kernel then reads. Since the monomial kernel already accesses the MonomialHeader (which contains `num_terms`/`term_offset`) and the required data (`d_eq_3bs`, `d_terms`) is already on the device, the combination can be computed inline with negligible overhead (~2-4 FpExt multiply-adds per monomial), eliminating the intermediate buffer allocation, the H2D/D2H traffic, and the precompute kernel launches entirely.\n"}}
