d_0 run back-to-back on the same stream, the 4.8MB M matrix likely stays in L2 cache (which is 72MB), so the read-back is nearly free. That means the fusion benefit shrinks to just the kernel launch overhead, which is only a few microseconds—not worth the added complexity.\n\nI should stop chasing marginal optimizations and focus on where the profiler actually shows GPU idle time. Looking at the stream synchronization data, there are over 1200 calls to cudaStreamSynchronize averaging 1.76ms each, which suggests there might be stalls between phases within a segment. of these syncs are in the GKR inner rounds (per-round D2H). Each D2H sync takes ~4μs (median from API summary). But some take much longer (max 55ms). The long syncs are when the CPU waits for GPU kernels to complete.\n\nThe 55ms max sync is likely at the end of a major phase (e.g., Round 0 barrier sync after all 8 threads complete). If there's a 55ms wait, that means the CPU thread reached the sync point 55ms before the GPU finished. During those 55ms, the CPU was blocked while the GPU was busy. No idle time to exploit.\n\nAfter all this analysis, I believe the honest and correct thing to do is to acknowledge that after 44 iterations, the remaining optimization space is very limited. The most practical approach is to target one of: replacing blocking transfers with non-blocking async copies to overlap CPU work with GPU execution, improving occupancy in underutilized kernels like `bit_rev_frac_build_k2_kernel`, or optimizing the WHIR algebraic batching kernel for wide matrices.\n\nI'm going to focus on option 3 and optimize the WHIR algebraic batching kernel since it processes 106K columns at APC 300 and takes ~3ms per call across 6 instances. The first WHIR round's batching is where I can make the most impact.\n\nLooking at the kernel's memory access pattern, if it's bandwidth-limited when reading 106K columns per row, I can vectorize the loads, tile the row processing for better cache reuse, and use shar