til the tree build finishes. The M matrix can't be computed incrementally from a partial tree. I need to stop exploring dead ends and commit to an actual optimization strategy.\n\nI'm zeroing in on the Stacked Reduction MLE rounds as the target—they're scaling 4x worse than the main MLE rounds and consuming 24ms per segment at APC 300. These rounds use a different kernel than the problematic ROUND 0 phase that failed with multi-streaming before, so maybe applying multi-streaming here could work without hitting the same accumulation buffer issues.\n\nActually, wait—the Stacked Reduction processes a single polynomial in one batch call, not independent trace groups. There's no way to split this into separate streams without fundamentally changing how the sumcheck operates. That won't work.\n\nLet me pivot to the WHIR fold buffer arena optimization instead. The previous attempt was reverted over a simple .len() bug, not because the core idea was flawed. If I implement it correctly using the non-owning view pattern that's already proven elsewhere, the savings should be real.\n\nBut hold on—those fold buffers are only 32-65 KB, and at that size cudaMallocAsync already uses pool memory with negligible overhead. Pre-allocation would save maybe 6 microseconds at most. The actual WHIR regression is coming from the global VPMM page size change, not the allocation itself.\n\nI need to step back and think about what would actually move the needle. After all this exploration, the real opportunity is looking at the `precompute_m_build_partial_kernel` in the GKR inner rounds from scratch. At 22.5ms per segment, it's the second-biggest cost there, and understanding whether there's a fundamentally faster algorithm for what it computes would be the most impactful direction to pursue.\n\nThat said, I need to deliver an implementation plan, not a research roadmap. So here's what I'm actually going to do: overlap the Stacked Reduction with WHIR's algebraic batching by starting the batching before Stacked Reduction finishes. The challenge is that algebraic batching needs the random coefficient mu, which normally gets sampled from the transcript after Stacked Reduction completes. But if I sample mu earlier—right from the transcript state before Stacked Reduction—I can kick off the batching work in parallel.\n\nLooking at the proof flow, though, the transcript gets built from Stacked Reduction's outputs, so mu can't actually be sampled beforehand without restructuring the entire transcript and verifier logic. That's off the table given the constraints.\n\nSo my actual final plan is to optimize Trace Commit by skipping RS codeword computation for any AIRs that have empty traces—some autoprecompile AIRs at APC 300 might have zero rows, so there's no point computing those codewords.\n\nActually, wait—autoprecompile AIRs with zero traces wouldn't even be included in the proving run, so there are no zero-trace AIRs to optimize for here.\n\nThat's not viable either. Let me refocus on the WHIR arena issue. The fold buffers are small (32-65 KB), so the regression probably isn't just about those. The VPMM page size change might be affecting how WHIR's sumcheck buffers get allocated. I need to look at what else happens during WHIR—the algebraic batching reads from cached RS codewords, iNTT runs in-place on the batched polynomial, MLE interpolation does in-place operations, and then sumcheck rounds use those fold buffers.\n\nThe Merkle tree construction is allocating new DeviceBuffers for each digest layer. At APC 300, that's roughly 1 MB for the leaf digests, 512 KB for layer 1, 256 KB for layer 2, and so on—all well below the 16 MiB VPMM threshold. Before the page size change, these buffers went through VPMM, but now they're going through cudaMallocAsync instead, which might be causing the performance shift.\n\nI'm thinking about pre-allocating a single 16 MiB VPMM buffer as an arena for all the Merkle tree digest allocations across the WHIR rounds. This would