{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_013THRdw28Xk9C3SsfMXf3yt","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_012VgBG9CaT6kqpHqeaUyrYJ","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/2026-04-16-1100-whir-vpmm-arena-fold-buffers/plan.md","content":"# Plan: WHIR VPMM Arena for Merkle Tree Digest Allocations\n\n## Goal\n\nRecover the ~25ms WHIR regression at APC 300 (125ms current vs 100ms baseline) by routing WHIR Merkle tree digest layer allocations through a pre-allocated VPMM-backed arena. When the VPMM page size was increased from 2 MiB to 16 MiB (task vpmm-bulk-page-creation), allocations below 16 MiB shifted from VPMM (cuMemCreate + cuMemMap managed physical pages) to cudaMallocAsync (CUDA memory pool). The WHIR Merkle tree digest layers (256 KB - 1 MB each) were among these. The global page size tuning reports (tune-vpmm-page-size-for-whir, decouple-vpmm-pool-threshold) confirmed that any global change that recovers WHIR causes a larger GKR regression due to VPMM pool state sensitivity. This plan uses a targeted approach: one 16 MiB VPMM-backed arena allocated before WHIR, with digest layers sub-allocated as non-owning views.\n\n## Current Code Path\n\n### Merkle tree construction (`crates/cuda-backend/src/merkle_tree.rs:113-172`)\n\n`MerkleTreeGpu::new_generic_with_hash<MH>()` allocates digest layers via `DeviceBuffer::<D>::with_capacity()`:\n\n1. **Line 127**: `query_digest_layer = DeviceBuffer::<D>::with_capacity(query_stride)` — first layer (leaf digests). At APC 300 with WHIR height ~32K: 32K × 32 bytes = **1 MB**.\n2. **Lines 143-159**: Loop allocating subsequent layers via `DeviceBuffer::<D>::with_capacity(prev_layer.len() / 2)`. Each layer is half the previous. For a 32K-leaf tree: 512 KB, 256 KB, 128 KB, ... down to 32 bytes (root). Total: ~17 layers, ~2 MB.\n3. All layers stored in `digest_layers: Vec<DeviceBuffer<D>>` (line 142). These are kept alive for later WHIR query operations (`batch_query_proofs` at lines 278-355).\n\n### Allocation routing (`crates/cuda-common/src/memory_manager/mod.rs:71-98`)\n\n`d_malloc(size)` routes: if `size < self.pool.page_size` (16 MiB), use `cudaMallocAsync`. Otherwise, use VPMM. All WHIR Merkle digest layers are < 16 MiB → **cudaMallocAsync**.\n\nBefore the page size change (2 MiB pages): layers ≥ 2 MiB went through VPMM, layers < 2 MiB through cudaMallocAsync. Only the leaf layer (1 MB) was right at the boundary. After the change: ALL layers go through cudaMallocAsync.\n\n### WHIR usage (`crates/cuda-backend/src/whir.rs:62-531`)\n\nThe WHIR proving function creates multiple Merkle trees:\n- One per WHIR sumcheck round (typically 3 rounds per segment)\n- Each tree commits a folded polynomial\n- Trees are stored and later queried for opening proofs\n\n### Non-owning DeviceBuffer pattern (`crates/cuda-common/src/d_buffer.rs:44-80`)\n\nThe MLE fold optimization (active, commit `a36c3986`) successfully uses `DeviceBuffer::non_owning()` to create views into pre-allocated arenas. Non-owning buffers have `owns_memory: false` and skip `d_free()` on drop.\n\n## Changes\n\n### Change 1: Add `DeviceArena` helper type\n\n**File:** `crates/cuda-common/src/d_buffer.rs`\n\nAdd a simple bump allocator backed by a single `DeviceBuffer<u8>`:\n\n```rust\npub struct DeviceArena {\n    buffer: DeviceBuffer<u8>,\n    offset: usize,\n}\n\nimpl DeviceArena {\n    /// Create a new arena with `capacity_bytes` of VPMM-backed memory.\n    /// The capacity must be >= VPMM page_size (typically 16 MiB) to ensure\n    /// allocation goes through VPMM rather than cudaMallocAsync.\n    pub fn new(capacity_bytes: usize) -> Self {\n        Self {\n            buffer: DeviceBuffer::<u8>::with_capacity(capacity_bytes),\n            offset: 0,\n        }\n    }\n\n    /// Allocate `count` elements of type T from the arena, returning a non-owning DeviceBuffer.\n    /// Returns None if the arena doesn't have enough remaining capacity.\n    pub fn alloc<T: Copy>(&mut self, count: usize) -> Option<DeviceBuffer<T>> {\n        let size_bytes = std::mem::size_of::<T>() * count;\n        let align = std::mem::align_of::<T>();\n        let aligned_offset = self.offset.next_multiple_of(align);\n        if aligned_offset + size_bytes > self.buffer.len() {\n            return None; // Arena full\n        }\n        let ptr = unsafe { self.buffer.as_mut_ptr().add(aligned_offset) as *mut T };\n        self.offset = aligned_offset + size_bytes;\n        Some(unsafe { DeviceBuffer::non_owning(ptr, count) })\n    }\n\n    /// Reset the arena for reuse (does not free GPU memory).\n    pub fn reset(&mut self) {\n        self.offset = 0;\n    }\n}\n```\n\n**Why:** Provides a clean, reusable abstraction for arena allocation. The backing buffer goes through `DeviceBuffer::with_capacity()` → `d_malloc()` → VPMM (because capacity ≥ 16 MiB). Sub-allocations are non-owning views that don't trigger `d_free()` on drop.\n\n### Change 2: Add arena-aware Merkle tree constructor\n\n**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/src/merkle_tree.rs`\n\nAdd a new method `new_generic_with_hash_arena<MH>()` that accepts an optional `&mut DeviceArena`:\n\n```rust\nfn new_generic_with_hash_arena<MH: GpuMerkleHash<Digest = D>>(\n    matrix: DeviceMatrix<F>,\n    rows_per_query: usize,\n    cache_backing_matrix: bool,\n    arena: Option<&mut DeviceArena>,\n) -> Result<Self, MerkleTreeError> {\n    // ... same as new_generic_with_hash, but replace:\n    // DeviceBuffer::<D>::with_capacity(size) \n    // with:\n    // arena.as_mut().and_then(|a| a.alloc::<D>(size))\n    //     .unwrap_or_else(|| DeviceBuffer::<D>::with_capacity(size))\n}\n```\n\nThe existing `new_generic_with_hash()` delegates to the new function with `arena: None` (preserving behavior for non-WHIR callers).\n\n**Why:** The Merkle tree constructor is the only place where digest layers are allocated. By optionally routing these allocations through an arena, we can control the physical memory backing without affecting any other code path.\n\n### Change 3: Pre-allocate WHIR arena before the WHIR proving function\n\n**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/src/whir.rs`\n\nAt the start of `prove_whir_opening_gpu()` (line ~62), before the WHIR outer loop:\n\n```rust\n// Pre-allocate a 16 MiB arena for WHIR Merkle tree digest layers.\n// This forces digest allocations through VPMM (cuMemCreate + cuMemMap)\n// rather than cudaMallocAsync, restoring the physical memory layout\n// that existed before the VPMM page size increase.\nlet mut whir_arena = DeviceArena::new(16 * 1024 * 1024); // 16 MiB\n```\n\nThen pass `Some(&mut whir_arena)` to each Merkle tree construction within the WHIR loop. Reset the arena between WHIR rounds (since each round's Merkle tree is queried and can be dropped before the next round).\n\nWait — the Merkle trees are stored and queried later (for opening proofs). If digest layers are non-owning views into the arena, they must remain valid as long as the Merkle tree is alive. The arena must not be reset or freed while any Merkle tree referencing it is still alive.\n\nThis means the arena must live at least as long as all WHIR Merkle trees. Since the trees are consumed within the WHIR proving function (proofs are extracted, then trees are dropped), the arena can be scoped to the function.\n\n**Why:** A single 16 MiB allocation goes through VPMM. All digest sub-allocations are non-owning views into this contiguous physical memory. This restores the pre-regression physical memory layout for WHIR hash/compress kernels.\n\n### Change 4: Thread arena through the `MerkleTreeConstructor` trait\n\n**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/src/hash_scheme.rs`\n\nThe `MerkleTreeConstructor` trait has `new_merkle_tree()` which doesn't accept an arena. Add an `Option<&mut DeviceArena>` parameter:\n\n```rust\npub trait MerkleTreeConstructor {\n    type Digest: ...;\n    \n    fn new_merkle_tree(\n        matrix: DeviceMatrix<F>,\n        rows_per_query: usize,\n        cache_backing_matrix: bool,\n        arena: Option<&mut DeviceArena>,\n    ) -> Result<MerkleTreeGpu<F, Self::Digest>, MerkleTreeError>;\n}\n```\n\nUpdate the `Poseidon2MerkleHash` implementation to pass the arena through to `new_generic_with_hash_arena()`.\n\nUpdate `MerkleTreeGpu::new_with_hash()` to accept and forward the arena parameter.\n\n**Why:** The arena must be threaded through the trait boundary to reach the allocation site in `new_generic_with_hash_arena()`.\n\n### Change 5: Update all non-WHIR callers to pass `None` for arena\n\n**Files:**\n- `crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_pcs.rs` — Trace Commit's Merkle tree construction\n- Any other callers of `MerkleTreeGpu::new_with_hash()` or `MerkleTreeConstructor::new_merkle_tree()`\n\nPass `arena: None` to preserve existing behavior. Only WHIR passes a `Some(arena)`.\n\n**Why:** Ensures no other phase's allocation patterns change. The optimization is targeted to WHIR only.\n\n## Invariants\n\n1. **Correctness**: Merkle tree digest values must be identical whether allocated via arena or via `DeviceBuffer::with_capacity()`. The non-owning views have the same device pointer semantics — only the allocation routing differs. Verify by running all 94 tests.\n\n2. **Arena lifetime**: The arena must outlive all Merkle trees whose digest layers are sub-allocated from it. The arena is scoped to `prove_whir_opening_gpu()` and all Merkle trees are consumed within this function. Verify by checking that no `MerkleTreeGpu` holding arena-backed digest layers escapes the function.\n\n3. **No regression at APC 0**: At APC 0, WHIR is 218ms (unchanged from baseline). The arena adds one 16 MiB VPMM allocation at the start of WHIR. This is negligible overhead. Verify empirically.\n\n4. **Non-owning buffer drop safety**: Non-owning `DeviceBuffer` instances (with `owns_memory: false`) must NOT call `d_free()` on drop. This is already guaranteed by the existing `non_owning()` implementation (line 44-80 of `d_buffer.rs`).\n\n5. **Arena capacity**: The 16 MiB arena must be large enough for all WHIR Merkle digest layers across all rounds within a segment. At APC 300 with 3 WHIR rounds and max tree height 32K: total digest memory = 3 × ~2 MB = ~6 MB. 16 MiB is sufficient. If a tree requires more than 16 MiB of digest space (unlikely for this benchmark), the `alloc()` fallback returns `None` and the normal `DeviceBuffer::with_capacity()` path is used.\n\n## Measurement Plan\n\n1. Build: `cargo build --bin powdr_openvm_riscv -r --features \"metrics,cuda\"` (from powdr repo)\n2. Run APC 0, 100, 300 benchmarks:\n   ```\n   powdr_openvm_riscv prove --artifact apcXXX.cbor --input 0 --metrics <output>.json --recursion\n   ```\n3. Analyze with `spec.py` — focus on:\n   - WHIR time at APC 300 (expect: 100-115ms, down from 125ms)\n   - STARK excl trace at APC 300 (expect: 1100-1115ms, down from 1125ms)\n   - APC 0 WHIR (expect: no regression, ~218ms)\n   - LogUp GKR at APC 300 (expect: no regression, ~369ms)\n4. Run nsight profile for APC 300 to verify:\n   - poseidon2_compressing_row_hashes and merkle_tree_compress kernel times are reduced for WHIR trees\n   - No increase in cuMemCreate calls (the arena is one VPMM allocation)\n5. Run full test suite: `cargo nextest run -p openvm-cuda-backend --test-threads=4`\n\n## Rollback Criteria\n\n- Revert if WHIR improvement at APC 300 is < 5ms\n- Revert if LogUp GKR at APC 300 regresses by > 10ms\n- Revert if APC 0 STARK excl trace regresses by > 20ms\n- Revert if any test fails\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":917,"cache_read_input_tokens":233688,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":917},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"78bbe79a-49aa-4ea3-b7e0-2913b403757d","uuid":"85a1f211-474e-4364-8e9b-2e93f48a9c83"}
