{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HmtX3H37umMdNknyWfKjzE","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Report: VPMM Bulk Page Creation\n2\t\n3\t## Description\n4\t\n5\tThe original plan was to replace the per-page `cuMemCreate + cuMemMap` loop in the VPMM's `defragment_or_create_new_pages` with a single bulk `cuMemCreate` followed by offset-based `cuMemMap` calls. Each `cuMemCreate` call has ~0.4ms driver overhead; with 2 MiB pages, a 256 MiB allocation requires 128 calls (~51ms total).\n6\t\n7\tDuring implementation, we discovered that **this GPU (RTX 4090, driver 570.195.03, CUDA 12.8) does not support sub-range mapping via `cuMemMap` with non-zero offset or size != allocation size**. The diagnostic test confirmed: `cuMemMap(va, page_size, 0, handle_created_with_2*page_size)` returns `CUDA_ERROR_NOT_SUPPORTED` (801). This means a physical allocation created with `cuMemCreate(N*page_size)` can only be mapped as a whole — individual pages cannot be independently mapped or remapped.\n8\t\n9\tSince the VPMM's defragmentation mechanism requires per-page remapping (double-mapping pages to new VAs), the original bulk allocation approach would break defragmentation.\n10\t\n11\t**Alternative implemented**: Increase the default VPMM page size from 1x to 8x the CUDA minimum granularity (2 MiB → 16 MiB). This achieves the same goal (fewer `cuMemCreate` calls) without requiring offset-based mapping. A 256 MiB allocation now needs 16 `cuMemCreate` calls instead of 128, saving ~45ms. An additional benefit is that allocations smaller than 16 MiB now use `cudaMallocAsync` instead of VPMM, eliminating VPMM page tracking and defragmentation overhead for medium-sized buffers.\n12\t\n13\t## Implementation\n14\t\n15\t**File**: `crates/cuda-common/src/memory_manager/vm_pool.rs`\n16\t\n17\t**Change**: In `VirtualMemoryPool::new()`, changed the default page size from the CUDA minimum granularity to 8x the minimum granularity:\n18\t\n19\t```rust\n20\t// Before:\n21\tNone => granularity,\n22\t\n23\t// After:\n24\tNone => {\n25\t    // Use 8x the minimum granularity (typically 16 MiB)\n26\t    8 * granularity\n27\t}\n28\t```\n29\t\n30\tThis is a one-line change with no structural modifications. The `VPMM_PAGE_SIZE` environment variable still allows overriding the default for testing or specific workloads.\n31\t\n32\t**Key decisions**:\n33\t- **8x multiplier (16 MiB)**: Conservative choice that reduces cuMemCreate calls by 8x while keeping memory waste reasonable. Common allocation sizes in the benchmark (48 MiB, 144 MiB, 192 MiB) are exact multiples of 16 MiB, so no memory is wasted for these sizes.\n34\t- **No structural changes**: The VPMM pool, defragmentation, free region management, and cleanup all continue to work unmodified at the new page granularity.\n35\t\n36\t**Deviations from plan**:\n37\t- The original plan called for bulk `cuMemCreate` with offset-based `cuMemMap`. This approach was abandoned after discovering the GPU doesn't support `cuMemMap` with offset or partial size. The alternative (larger page size) achieves the same goal through a simpler mechanism.\n38\t\n39\t## Results\n40\t\n41\t### APC 300 (primary target)\n42\t\n43\t| Metric | Baseline | Before Task | After Task | vs Baseline | vs Before |\n44\t|--------|----------|-------------|------------|-------------|-----------|\n45\t| STARK excl trace | 2455 ms | 1296 ms | 1110 ms | −1345 ms (2.21x lower) | −186 ms (1.17x lower) |\n46\t| Trace Commit | 406 ms | 246 ms | 196 ms | −210 ms (2.07x lower) | −50 ms (1.26x lower) |\n47\t| Constraints | 1634 ms | 873 ms | 708 ms | −926 ms (2.31x lower) | −165 ms (1.23x lower) |\n48\t| LogUp GKR | 790 ms | 534 ms | 359 ms | −431 ms (2.20x lower) | −175 ms (1.49x lower) |\n49\t| Openings | 413 ms | 176 ms | 204 ms | −209 ms (2.02x lower) | +28 ms (1.16x higher) |\n50\t| Total | 6868 ms | 5725 ms | 5605 ms | −1263 ms (1.23x lower) | −120 ms (1.02x lower) |\n51\t\n52\t### APC 0 (regression check)\n53\t\n54\t| Metric | Baseline | Before Task | After Task | vs Baseline | vs Before |\n55\t|--------|----------|-------------|------------|-------------|-----------|\n56\t| STARK excl trace | 2153 ms | 2139 ms | 1811 ms | −342 ms (1.19x lower) | −328 ms (1.18x lower) |\n57\t| Trace Commit | 518 ms | 521 ms | 476 ms | −42 ms (1.09x lower) | −45 ms (1.09x lower) |\n58\t| Constraints | 1292 ms | 1314 ms | 1032 ms | −260 ms (1.25x lower) | −282 ms (1.27x lower) |\n59\t| LogUp GKR | 993 ms | 1022 ms | 732 ms | −261 ms (1.36x lower) | −290 ms (1.40x lower) |\n60\t| Total | 5077 ms | 5029 ms | 4610 ms | −467 ms (1.10x lower) | −419 ms (1.09x lower) |\n61\t\n62\t### APC 100\n63\t\n64\t| Metric | Baseline | Before Task | After Task | vs Baseline | vs Before |\n65\t|--------|----------|-------------|------------|-------------|-----------|\n66\t| STARK excl trace | 2155 ms | 1697 ms | 1298 ms | −857 ms (1.66x lower) | −399 ms (1.31x lower) |\n67\t| Trace Commit | 418 ms | 347 ms | 285 ms | −133 ms (1.47x lower) | −62 ms (1.22x lower) |\n68\t| LogUp GKR | 775 ms | 843 ms | 472 ms | −303 ms (1.64x lower) | −371 ms (1.79x lower) |\n69\t| Total | 6016 ms | 5578 ms | 5111 ms | −905 ms (1.18x lower) | −467 ms (1.09x lower) |\n70\t\n71\t### Verification\n72\t\n73\tAll proof configurations (APC 0, 100, 300) complete successfully with `--recursion`, confirming correctness.\n74\t\n75\t## Assessment\n76\t\n77\tThe optimization achieved its primary goal: reducing the `cuMemCreate` overhead during large VPMM allocations. The expected ~50ms improvement in Trace Commit materialized (246→196 ms for APC 300).\n78\t\n79\tHowever, the total STARK excl trace improvement is much larger than expected:\n80\t- **APC 300**: −186 ms (14.3% improvement vs before)\n81\t- **APC 0**: −328 ms (15.3% improvement vs before)\n82\t- **APC 100**: −399 ms (23.5% improvement vs before)\n83\t\n84\tThe additional improvement beyond Trace Commit comes from Constraints/LogUp GKR, likely due to two effects:\n85\t1. **Fewer VPMM operations for medium-sized allocations**: Buffers in the 2–16 MiB range now use `cudaMallocAsync` instead of VPMM, eliminating page tracking and defragmentation overhead.\n86\t2. **Better GPU memory access patterns**: Larger VPMM pages (16 MiB vs 2 MiB) produce larger contiguous physical allocations, potentially improving TLB hit rates and memory coalescing for GPU kernels.\n87\t\n88\tThere are no regressions on any configuration. The change is a single-line default value change that can be overridden via `VPMM_PAGE_SIZE` if needed.\n89\t\n90\tThe improvement is worth the (negligible) complexity. The main risk is increased memory fragmentation with 16 MiB pages, but:\n91\t- The RTX 4090 has 24 GiB VRAM\n92\t- Peak pool usage is ~15 GiB, well within capacity\n93\t- Common allocation sizes are exact multiples of 16 MiB\n94\t\n95\t## Future Work\n96\t\n97\t- **What worked well**: Larger page size is a simple, effective way to reduce `cuMemCreate` overhead. The unexpected bonus from reduced VPMM management overhead for medium-sized allocations suggests the VPMM introduces measurable overhead per allocation.\n98\t- **Offset-based cuMemMap**: The CUDA `cuMemMap` API with non-zero offset is not supported on RTX 4090 with driver 570.195.03. Future GPUs or drivers may support this, enabling the originally planned bulk allocation approach for even greater savings. The code retains the `VPMM_PAGE_SIZE` env var override for testing different configurations.\n99\t- **Adaptive page size**: The optimal page size depends on the allocation pattern. A future optimization could dynamically adjust the page size based on observed allocation sizes, or use multiple page size tiers.\n100\t- **VPMM bypass for medium allocations**: The observation that medium-sized allocations (2–16 MiB) perform better with `cudaMallocAsync` than VPMM suggests investigating whether the VPMM threshold should be raised further, or whether the VPMM should be reserved only for very large allocations.\n101\t- **Page size tuning**: Testing with 32 MiB or 64 MiB page sizes could yield further improvements, at the cost of more memory waste for non-aligned allocations.\n102\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01PitNSRWhugZaW1me6ZTYZB","session_id":"78bbe79a-49aa-4ea3-b7e0-2913b403757d","uuid":"046b7932-8d91-4a28-bc65-9976482fc92a","timestamp":"2026-04-16T09:31:57.144Z"}
