{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FfDGpy6zDh23nDWvom3op7","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/task.md has been updated successfully."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"78bbe79a-49aa-4ea3-b7e0-2913b403757d","uuid":"e2bb54c4-cc5a-444d-bba6-f6b5cc3ce4ac","timestamp":"2026-04-16T09:37:33.446Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"update","filePath":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/task.md","content":"# 2026-04-16-1100-tune-gkr-precompute-m-parameters\n\nTune the GKR fractional sumcheck PrecomputeM strategy parameters (`TARGET_BLOCKS`, `MIN_BLOCKS`, `MIN_N`, `TAIL_TILE`) to reduce the GKR inner round cost at APC 300. The GKR inner rounds are the single largest sequential component at 213ms (107ms per segment), with `precompute_m_build_partial` at 45ms and `multifold` at 47ms being the dominant kernels. The current defaults (`TARGET_BLOCKS=1024`, `MIN_BLOCKS=64`, `MIN_N=22`, `TAIL_TILE=4096`) were set without specific tuning for the APC 300 workload, which has a different GKR tree shape (more traces, smaller per-trace) than APC 0. Parameter sweeping via the existing `SWIRL_CUDA_GKR_PRECOMPUTE_M_*` environment variables can identify a configuration that better balances GPU parallelism and per-window overhead for this workload.\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":1,"oldLines":3,"newStart":1,"newLines":3,"lines":["-# 2026-04-16-1100-whir-vpmm-arena-fold-buffers","+# 2026-04-16-1100-tune-gkr-precompute-m-parameters"," ","-Recover the ~25ms WHIR regression at APC 300 by routing WHIR Merkle tree digest layer allocations through a pre-allocated VPMM-backed arena. When the VPMM page size was increased from 2 MiB to 16 MiB (task vpmm-bulk-page-creation), WHIR's Merkle tree digest layers (256 KB - 1 MB each, ~6 MB total per segment) shifted from VPMM (cuMemCreate/cuMemMap physical pages) to cudaMallocAsync (CUDA memory pool). This changed the physical memory layout for subsequent bandwidth-sensitive Poseidon2 hashing and compression kernels. Previous global page size tuning attempts failed because GKR regressed more than WHIR improved. This approach is targeted: allocate one 16 MiB VPMM-backed buffer before WHIR, sub-allocate digest layers as non-owning views within it, and free the arena after WHIR completes. No other phase's allocations are affected.","+Tune the GKR fractional sumcheck PrecomputeM strategy parameters (`TARGET_BLOCKS`, `MIN_BLOCKS`, `MIN_N`, `TAIL_TILE`) to reduce the GKR inner round cost at APC 300. The GKR inner rounds are the single largest sequential component at 213ms (107ms per segment), with `precompute_m_build_partial` at 45ms and `multifold` at 47ms being the dominant kernels. The current defaults (`TARGET_BLOCKS=1024`, `MIN_BLOCKS=64`, `MIN_N=22`, `TAIL_TILE=4096`) were set without specific tuning for the APC 300 workload, which has a different GKR tree shape (more traces, smaller per-trace) than APC 0. Parameter sweeping via the existing `SWIRL_CUDA_GKR_PRECOMPUTE_M_*` environment variables can identify a configuration that better balances GPU parallelism and per-window overhead for this workload."]}],"originalFile":"# 2026-04-16-1100-whir-vpmm-arena-fold-buffers\n\nRecover the ~25ms WHIR regression at APC 300 by routing WHIR Merkle tree digest layer allocations through a pre-allocated VPMM-backed arena. When the VPMM page size was increased from 2 MiB to 16 MiB (task vpmm-bulk-page-creation), WHIR's Merkle tree digest layers (256 KB - 1 MB each, ~6 MB total per segment) shifted from VPMM (cuMemCreate/cuMemMap physical pages) to cudaMallocAsync (CUDA memory pool). This changed the physical memory layout for subsequent bandwidth-sensitive Poseidon2 hashing and compression kernels. Previous global page size tuning attempts failed because GKR regressed more than WHIR improved. This approach is targeted: allocate one 16 MiB VPMM-backed buffer before WHIR, sub-allocate digest layers as non-owning views within it, and free the arena after WHIR completes. No other phase's allocations are affected.\n"}}
